WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
AI-assisted review should support pull request review, not bypass it.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:11.880
A practical workflow begins when the developer opens a pull request and describes the security-relevant intent of the change.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:19.660
AI can summarize changed routes, configuration, dependencies, tests, and possible security questions.

4
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:26.320
The reviewer then asks targeted prompts, validates findings, and decides which comments are worth raising.

5
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:32.820
The goal is to reduce blind spots and review fatigue, not to flood developers with generic warnings.

6
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:43.820
CI/CD should continue to run normal controls: tests, linting, static analysis, dependency scanning, secret scanning, policy checks, and deployment gates.

7
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:52.880
AI can help interpret output, draft remediation guidance, suggest tests, and organize findings, but automated tools remain necessary evidence sources.

8
00:00:52.980 --> 00:01:06.020
High-risk changes may still require security-owner review, architecture review, or approval gates, especially when they affect authentication, authorization, secrets, public APIs, or production deployment behavior.

9
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:12.380
Teams should document review criteria so AI-assisted comments align with local standards.

10
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:23.040
A reusable prompt template can ask for scope, trust boundaries, Node.js review priorities, evidence requirements, and verification steps.

11
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:30.740
Recurring findings should feed back into secure coding guidance, helper libraries, route templates, middleware patterns, and test examples.

12
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:39.640
AI assistance becomes more valuable when it reinforces the team's engineering system instead of acting as a one-off reviewer with no memory of standards.
